# Later — v2 scope

**Status:** Not started · **Tasks:** TASKS.md "Later — v2 scope" · **Depends on:** v1 shipped (M11 exit criteria met) · **Date:** 2026-11-01

v1 does one thing well: deterministic one-to-one matching of two CSV sources, with a safe investigation workflow. Real payment reconciliation is messier. Settlements are aggregated, fees are deducted, money arrives days later, a third source (the bank) has the final word, and data arrives without anyone uploading it.

This file sketches each v2 item well enough to plan it, estimate it and talk about it in an interview. It is **not** a build plan: each item becomes its own phase plan, in the same format as M0–M11, when you pick it up.

**Two rules for every item:**

1. **Update `docs/reconciliation-rules.md` first, then write an ADR.** Every item below changes what "match" means or where data comes from. If the rule isn't written down before the code, the tests have nothing to test against.
2. **v1 ships first.** None of these is worth starting while the v1 demo (upload → run → resolve → report) isn't working end to end and deployed. A finished v1 is a stronger portfolio piece than a half-built v2.

---

## 1. Dependency order

Some items change foundations that others build on. Build them in this order:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    T[2. Transaction types<br/>refunds, chargebacks, fees] --> F[3. Fee-aware matching]
    T --> A[1. One-to-many /<br/>many-to-one matching]
    W[4. Settlement-date windows] --> A
    A --> B[5. Bank statement<br/>three-way]
    F --> B
    M[6. Manual match / unmatch] --> A
    C[7. Per-org column mappings] --> S[8. Scheduled imports<br/>SFTP, object storage, API pulls]
    N[9. Notifications]
    U[10. Minimal web UI]
    R[11. Redis: only if measured]
```

| Order | Item | Why here |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Transaction types (§2) | Every later matching rule needs to know whether a row is a payment, refund, chargeback or fee |
| 2 | Settlement-date windows (§4) | Small, self-contained, and the aggregation work needs it |
| 3 | Manual match / unmatch (§6) | Gives investigators a way out of ambiguous cases before the automatic rules get more complex |
| 4 | Fee-aware matching (§3) | Needs fee rows to be typed |
| 5 | One-to-many / many-to-one (§1) | The biggest engine change; needs types, windows and manual match in place |
| 6 | Bank statement, three-way (§5) | Builds on aggregation: a bank deposit is almost always an aggregate |
| — | Column mappings (§7) → scheduled imports (§8) | An independent track. Automated pulls need per-org mappings, because nobody is there to fix a header |
| — | Notifications (§9), web UI (§10) | Independent; can be done any time after v1 |
| — | Redis (§11) | Only when a measurement says so. It may never happen |

Sections below follow TASKS.md's order, not build order.

---

## 2. Shared schema facts to know before starting

V1's schema already anticipates some of this, and blocks some of it:

| Fact | Effect on v2 |
|---|---|
| `transaction_record.transaction_type` CHECK already allows `PAYMENT`, `REFUND`, `CHARGEBACK`, `FEE` | §2 needs no schema change for the types themselves |
| `transaction_record.source_type` CHECK allows only `INTERNAL_LEDGER`, `GATEWAY` | §5 needs a migration to add `BANK` (on `import_batch` and `reconciliation_run`'s source columns too) |
| `reconciliation_match` has `UNIQUE (run_id, ledger_record_id)` and `UNIQUE (run_id, gateway_record_id)`, with one ledger and one gateway column | §1 can't fit in this table. It needs a match-group model (see §1) |
| `reconciliation_match.match_type` CHECK allows only `EXACT` | §1, §3, §6 extend it |
| `reconciliation_match.amount_difference` exists and is always 0 for `EXACT` | §3 uses it for the fee |
| Applied migrations are never edited | Every change above is a new migration |

---

## 3. The items

### 3.1 One-to-many and many-to-one matching (aggregated settlements)

**The business problem.** A gateway rarely pays out each payment separately. It sends one settlement line, "payout PO-5521: 48,250.00 AED", covering 312 customer payments from yesterday. The ledger has 312 rows; the gateway file has one. v1 reports 312 `MISSING_IN_GATEWAY` cases and one `MISSING_IN_LEDGER`, which is technically true and practically useless.

The reverse (many-to-one) also happens: one large order paid in instalments, recorded once in the ledger and several times by the gateway.

**Design sketch.**

- **Linking key.** Aggregates are only matchable if something links the parts to the whole. Gateways usually provide it: a `payout_id` / `settlement_id` column on each payment row, or a separate settlement report listing which payments are in which payout. Without a linking key, the problem becomes subset-sum (see "hard parts"). **Find out what the target gateways actually provide before designing anything.**
- **Schema** (new migration):

```sql
-- A match is now a group with members on each side, not a pair.
CREATE TABLE reconciliation_match_group (
    id                 UUID          PRIMARY KEY,
    organization_id    UUID          NOT NULL REFERENCES organization (id),
    run_id             UUID          NOT NULL REFERENCES reconciliation_run (id),
    match_type         TEXT          NOT NULL CHECK (match_type IN ('EXACT', 'AGGREGATE', 'FEE_ADJUSTED', 'MANUAL')),
    left_total         NUMERIC(19,4) NOT NULL,
    right_total        NUMERIC(19,4) NOT NULL,
    amount_difference  NUMERIC(19,4) NOT NULL,
    created_at         TIMESTAMPTZ   NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE TABLE reconciliation_match_member (
    group_id               UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES reconciliation_match_group (id),
    run_id                 UUID NOT NULL,                      -- denormalized for the unique key
    organization_id        UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES organization (id),
    transaction_record_id  UUID NOT NULL,
    transaction_date       DATE NOT NULL,
    side                   TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (side IN ('LEFT', 'RIGHT')),
    PRIMARY KEY (group_id, transaction_record_id),
    UNIQUE (run_id, transaction_record_id),                    -- still: a record is matched once per run
    FOREIGN KEY (transaction_record_id, transaction_date) REFERENCES transaction_record (id, transaction_date)
);
```

  Keep `reconciliation_match` for v1's `EXACT` pairs, or migrate it into groups of size 1+1. Migrating gives one model for reports; keeping it avoids touching working code. Decide in the ADR. If you migrate, use the expand/contract pattern (M11): write both, backfill, switch reads, then drop.

- **Engine.** A new `MatchingStrategy`, `PayoutAggregateStrategy`, running **after** `ExactReferenceStrategy` on the records it left unmatched. Set-based, like v1: `GROUP BY payout_id` on the ledger side (sum of payments) joined to the gateway's payout line. Equal totals → `AGGREGATE` group. Unequal → a new exception type `AGGREGATE_MISMATCH` linking every member.
- **Strategy ordering becomes a rule.** "Exact first, then aggregate, then fee-adjusted" must be written in `reconciliation-rules.md`, and every record must still end up in exactly one place. The M6.19 property test extends to cover the new outcomes.
- **API.** Matches endpoint returns groups with members; the exception detail view handles hundreds of linked records (paginate them now; v1 capped at 500).

**Hard parts.**
- **No linking key → subset-sum.** "Which of these 312 payments add up to 48,250.00?" is NP-hard in general. Real systems avoid it: they require the linking key, or narrow candidates by date and merchant and accept only a unique solution, sending everything else to manual review (§6). Don't build a subset-sum solver.
- **Partial payouts.** The payout covers 311 of the 312 payments; one is held for risk review. That's an aggregate mismatch with a meaningful cause, so the exception needs to say *which* member is missing, not just "totals differ".
- **Idempotency.** The discrepancy key for an aggregate case must be stable across runs: key it on the payout reference (`AGGREGATE_MISMATCH:<payout_id>:<currency>`), not on member lists that can change.

**Decide or measure first.** What linking data do real gateway settlement reports contain? (Stripe payouts, Adyen settlement details, Razorpay settlements all differ.) What share of volume is aggregated?

**Interview angle.** "How would you match one bank deposit to hundreds of payments?" Group by a linking key and compare totals set-based; explain why the general version is subset-sum and NP-hard, and how real systems avoid it with linking keys, narrow candidate windows and manual review.

---

### 3.2 Refunds, chargebacks and fees as distinct transaction types

**The business problem.** v1 effectively reconciles payments. But a ledger also records refunds; a gateway file includes chargebacks (the customer's bank pulled money back) and fees. Treating all of them as `PAYMENT` produces wrong matches (a refund matching the original payment with the same reference) and wrong totals.

**Design sketch.**
- **Schema:** the types already exist in V1's CHECK. Add `related_reference` (nullable) to `transaction_record` in a new migration: a refund or chargeback points at the payment it reverses.
- **Import:** map source columns to types per source (`type=refund`, `txn_kind=CB`, …). With §7, the mapping is per organization.
- **Amounts:** M5 decided amounts are positive and direction comes from `transaction_type`. Write the sign table into `reconciliation-rules.md`: `PAYMENT +`, `REFUND −`, `CHARGEBACK −`, `FEE −` from the merchant's point of view. Reports apply the sign; storage never does.
- **Engine:** v1's match key already includes `transaction_type`, so a refund only matches a refund. New rules:
  - A chargeback in the gateway with no ledger entry → new exception type `UNRECORDED_CHARGEBACK` (common and important: money already left the account).
  - A refund whose `related_reference` has no payment in the ledger → `ORPHAN_REFUND`.
- **Reporting:** net settlement = payments − refunds − chargebacks − fees. M8's reports gain a by-type breakdown.

**Hard parts.**
- Chargebacks have their own lifecycle (opened, won, lost, reversed). Don't model it in v2; reconcile the money movements and leave dispute management out of scope.
- Partial refunds: one payment, several refunds. Matching refunds to refunds is still one-to-one by refund reference; the link to the payment is informational.

**Decide first.** The exact column values each target source uses for types, and the sign convention. Add sample CSVs for each type to `docs/samples/`.

**Interview angle.** "How do you represent refunds: negative amounts or a type?" A type plus a positive amount, with direction derived from the type in one place. Mixed sign conventions across sources are a classic source of reconciliation bugs.

---

### 3.3 Fee-aware matching (gross versus net settlement)

**The business problem.** The ledger records the customer paid 1,000.00. The gateway settles 971.00: 1,000.00 minus a 2.9% fee. v1 calls that an `AMOUNT_MISMATCH` every single time. With thousands of payments a day, the exception queue fills with noise, and real mismatches hide in it. (This is exactly PAY-103 in the worked example: 750 vs 700.)

**Design sketch.**
- **Two source shapes:**
  1. The gateway file has gross, fee and net columns per payment → compare ledger amount to **gross**, record fee as `amount_difference`. Easy and exact.
  2. The gateway file has only net → compare against an **expected fee** from a fee schedule, with a tolerance.
- **Schema:** `transaction_record.fee_amount NUMERIC(19,4)` and `gross_amount` (nullable) for shape 1. A new `fee_schedule` table for shape 2: `(organization_id, gateway, payment_method, percent NUMERIC(7,4), fixed NUMERIC(19,4), currency, valid_from, valid_to)`.
- **Engine:** new `FeeAdjustedStrategy` after exact matching. Shape 1: `ledger.amount = gateway.gross_amount` → `FEE_ADJUSTED` match with `amount_difference = fee`. Shape 2: `|ledger.amount − expected_fee − gateway.amount| ≤ tolerance` → `FEE_ADJUSTED`; outside tolerance → `AMOUNT_MISMATCH` with a reason code `FEE_OUTSIDE_TOLERANCE`.
- **Rounding:** the expected fee is computed with an explicit rounding mode (the gateway's, usually `HALF_UP` or `HALF_EVEN` to the currency's minor unit). Write it in `reconciliation-rules.md`; it's the kind of detail that produces 0.01 mismatches on 3% of rows.
- **Reporting:** total fees per run and per period become a first-class report: finance teams want to know what they paid.

**Hard parts.**
- **Tolerance is a business decision, not a technical one.** "Within 0.01" is defensible; "within 1%" will hide real problems. Make it per-organization configuration, audited when changed.
- Fee schedules change over time and per payment method. `valid_from`/`valid_to` and the payment's date pick the right one.
- Fees charged separately (a monthly fee line in the settlement) belong to §3.2's `FEE` type, not to per-payment matching.

**Decide first.** Which target gateways report gross/fee/net per row (shape 1 is far simpler)? What tolerance would a real finance team accept?

**Interview angle.** "How do you compare amounts that are supposed to differ?" Model the expected difference explicitly (a fee column or a schedule), compare with an explicit, documented tolerance and rounding mode, and record the difference on the match so it's auditable, never `Math.abs(a − b) < 0.5` hidden in code.

---

### 3.4 Configurable settlement-date windows

**The business problem.** A payment captured at 23:58 on 31 October settles on 2 November. A run for October finds the ledger record and no gateway record, and raises `MISSING_IN_GATEWAY`. A run for November raises `MISSING_IN_LEDGER` for the gateway record. Both are false positives.

**Design sketch.**
- v1's match key already excludes the date: two records match if both are **inside the run period** (phase-M1 §3). A window extends that: the **right** side's candidate range becomes `[period_start − before, period_end + after]`.
- **Configuration:** per organization and source pair, e.g. `gateway_settles_within_days = 3`. A table `reconciliation_settings (organization_id, left_source, right_source, window_before_days, window_after_days, …)`, changes audited.
- **Engine:** the classify query widens the right-side date filter. Partition pruning still works: it's still a date range.
- **Run record:** store the effective window on `reconciliation_run` (`window_before_days`, `window_after_days`) so a run's result can be explained later even if the setting changes.
- **Idempotency:** the run's derived idempotency key (M1) must include the window, or two runs with different windows would be considered "the same run".

**Hard parts.**
- **Double use across runs.** With a window, a gateway record dated 2 November can match a ledger record in the October run *and* be a candidate in the November run. Rule: in the November run, a right-side record already matched by a `COMPLETED` run is excluded from candidates. That rule needs a query across runs; index for it.
- Bigger windows mean more candidates and more chance of a false match on reused references. Keep windows small and per-gateway.

**Decide first.** The real settlement lag distribution for each gateway. Measure it from v1 data: for matched pairs, `gateway.transaction_date − ledger.transaction_date`.

**Interview angle.** "How do you reconcile data where the two sides arrive on different days?" A configurable, recorded tolerance window on one side, plus a rule preventing a record from being consumed by two runs; explain why the window must be part of the run's identity.

---

### 3.5 Bank statement as a third source (three-way reconciliation)

**The business problem.** The gateway says it paid out 48,250.00. Did the money actually arrive in the bank account? Gateway-to-ledger matching proves the gateway agrees with you; only the bank statement proves you have the money. Three-way reconciliation is ledger ↔ gateway ↔ bank.

**Design sketch.**
- **Schema:** add `BANK` to the `source_type` CHECKs (new migration; the V1 CHECKs are on `import_batch`, `transaction_record`, `reconciliation_run.left_source`/`right_source`). Bank rows carry the payout reference in the narrative/description field, usually unstructured.
- **Two runs, chained,** rather than one three-way run: run A matches ledger ↔ gateway (payments to payouts, via §3.1); run B matches gateway payouts ↔ bank deposits. A "three-way status" view joins them: a payment is fully reconciled when it's in a matched payout that's in a matched deposit.
- **Formats:** banks provide CSV exports, but also ISO 20022 `camt.053` (XML) and older MT940. Start with CSV through §3.7's mapping; add a `camt.053` parser as a separate import format later.
- **Reference extraction:** the payout ID is buried in text like `STRIPE PAYOUT PO_5521 REF 8812`. Extraction rules (regex per bank, per organization) become part of normalization and must keep the raw text.

**Hard parts.**
- **Fuzzy references.** Bank narratives are inconsistent. Exact matching on an extracted reference first, then amount + date-window candidates sent to manual review (§6). Don't auto-match on amount alone.
- **Bank-side aggregation.** One bank deposit may combine several payouts. That's §3.1 again, one level up.
- **Currency conversion.** A USD payout landing in an AED account needs an FX rate and a tolerance. That breaks the v1 "one currency per run" assumption; write an ADR before starting.

**Decide first.** Which bank export formats the target users have. Whether FX is in scope at all.

**Interview angle.** "What's three-way reconciliation and why does it matter?" Ledger vs processor proves the records agree; processor vs bank proves the cash arrived. Explain chaining two-way runs instead of one complex three-way join, and how reference extraction from unstructured bank text works.

---

### 3.6 Manual match and unmatch, with audit

**The business problem.** The engine is deliberately strict: duplicates and ambiguous cases become exceptions, never guesses. Some of those an investigator can resolve by saying "*this* ledger row and *that* gateway row are the same payment" (a typo in the reference, a reference reformatted by the gateway). Today they can only resolve the exception with a note; the match itself never exists, so reports keep counting both records as unmatched.

**Design sketch.**
- **API:**
  - `POST /api/v1/reconciliation-runs/{runId}/manual-matches` with `{ leftRecordIds: [...], rightRecordIds: [...], reason, exceptionId? }` → creates a `MANUAL` match group (§3.1's model).
  - `DELETE /api/v1/manual-matches/{id}` (or `POST …/unmatch`) with a reason → removes it.
- **Rules:**
  - Records must belong to the run, the organization, and not already be matched in that run (the `UNIQUE (run_id, transaction_record_id)` constraint enforces it).
  - Totals must be equal, or the difference must be explained with a reason code (`MANUAL_WITH_DIFFERENCE`).
  - The linked exception is resolved in the same transaction with resolution code `MANUALLY_MATCHED`.
  - Permission: a new `MATCH_MANUAL` (investigator, admin); unmatch: admin only. Four-eyes (one person proposes, another approves) is a common real-world control; consider it as a later addition.
- **Audit:** `MATCH_CREATED_MANUALLY`, `MATCH_REMOVED`, each with the full member list.
- **Re-runs:** a manual match must survive re-running the period. Rule: manual matches are carried forward: a new run for an overlapping period first copies `MANUAL` groups whose members are all in scope, then runs the automatic strategies on the rest.

**Hard parts.**
- **Carry-forward semantics** are the real design work. What if a carried-forward member's record was deleted by a re-import (§M5 retry)? The group becomes invalid; raise an exception rather than silently dropping it.
- Unmatching must reopen or recreate the exception it resolved, with history.

**Decide first.** Whether manual matches belong to a run or to the organization (global "these two records are the same" assertions). Per-run is simpler; global is what users expect after the second re-run. Write the ADR.

**Interview angle.** "How do you let humans override an automated decision without losing auditability?" A separate match type, explicit permissions, mandatory reasons, full audit of create and remove, and a defined rule for how overrides survive re-processing.

---

### 3.7 Per-organization configurable CSV column mappings

**The business problem.** v1 has two hard-coded CSV contracts (M1.3). Every gateway exports differently: `merchant_reference` vs `order_id` vs `Reference`; `dd/MM/yyyy` vs ISO dates; `1.234,56` vs `1,234.56`. Today an organization must reformat its files by hand before uploading.

**Design sketch.**
- **Schema:** `import_mapping (id, organization_id, name, source_type, version, definition JSONB, created_by, created_at)`. `definition` maps target fields to source columns and parsing options:

```json
{
  "columns": {
    "reference": "Order ID",
    "amount": "Net Amount",
    "currency": "Currency",
    "transaction_type": { "column": "Type", "values": { "charge": "PAYMENT", "refund": "REFUND" } },
    "status": { "column": "State", "values": { "paid": "SUCCESS", "failed": "FAILED" } },
    "transaction_at": "Created (UTC)"
  },
  "dateFormat": "dd/MM/yyyy HH:mm",
  "decimalSeparator": ",",
  "delimiter": ";",
  "encoding": "UTF-8"
}
```

- **Versioned and immutable:** editing a mapping creates a new version. `import_batch` records the `mapping_id` + `version` it was parsed with, so any batch can be explained and reprocessed exactly.
- **Validation:** a mapping is validated against a JSON Schema on save, and there's a "dry run" endpoint: upload a sample, get the first 20 parsed rows and any errors, no data written.
- **Code:** the M5 validator and normalizer take a `ColumnMapping` instead of hard-coded column names. If M5 kept the parsing behind an interface (`RowReader`), this is mostly configuration.

**Hard parts.**
- Mappings are user-supplied input that controls parsing. Validate strictly: no regex from users without limits (catastrophic backtracking is a denial-of-service), bounded number of columns.
- Changing a mapping must not change already-imported data. That's why versions are immutable.

**Decide first.** Collect 3–5 real-world export formats (public sample files from gateways' docs) and check that the mapping language covers them before building it.

**Interview angle.** "How do you support many input formats without a code change per customer?" Declarative, versioned mappings validated against a schema, recorded on every import for reproducibility, with a dry-run tool.

---

### 3.8 Scheduled imports from SFTP or object storage, and gateway API pulls

**The business problem.** Finance teams don't want to download a file every morning and upload it. Gateways drop settlement files on SFTP or into a bucket overnight, or offer an API.

**Design sketch.**
- **Connections:** `import_source (id, organization_id, kind SFTP|S3|GATEWAY_API, config JSONB, credentials_ref, mapping_id, schedule cron, enabled)`. Credentials are **never** stored in the database in plain text: `credentials_ref` points to a secret manager entry (or an encrypted column with a key outside the DB as the minimum).
- **Scheduling:** the M5 job table gains a `FETCH` job type. A scheduler (one per cluster, via a PostgreSQL advisory lock or ShedLock) enqueues `FETCH` jobs on each source's cron.
- **Fetch job:** list remote files → skip ones already fetched (a `fetched_file (import_source_id, remote_path, remote_etag_or_mtime, content_hash)` table) → download to `FileStorage` → create the `import_batch` and `IMPORT` job exactly as an upload would. The existing content-hash duplicate rule (409 in v1) becomes "skip and record" for automated sources.
- **API pulls:** per-gateway adapters behind one interface (`SettlementApiClient`), paging through settlements since the last cursor. Store the cursor per source; respect rate limits; retry transient errors with backoff (M9.3's classifier).
- **Audit:** `IMPORT_FETCHED` with source and remote path; the actor is the system, with `import_source_id` in the payload.

**Hard parts.**
- **Exactly-once fetch.** Download succeeded, crash before recording it → refetched next time. The content hash makes the second import a no-op, which is why the duplicate rule must be idempotent, not an error, for automated sources.
- **Partial files.** A file still being written to SFTP can be fetched half-done. Use the gateway's completion convention (a `.done` marker, a rename) or require the size to be stable across two listings.
- **Outbound network calls from workers** to customer-configured hosts are an SSRF risk: allowlist hosts, block private IP ranges.

**Depends on:** §3.7 (no human to fix headers when the import runs at 03:00).

**Interview angle.** "How do you build a reliable scheduled ingestion pipeline?" A single scheduler via a lock, idempotent fetch tracked by remote identity and content hash, reuse of the same import pipeline as manual uploads, secrets outside the database, and SSRF protection for user-configured endpoints.

---

### 3.9 Notifications: assignment and ageing past an SLA

**The business problem.** An exception is assigned and nobody notices. Another sits open for three weeks. Investigators shouldn't have to poll the list.

**Design sketch.**
- **Events:** `EXCEPTION_ASSIGNED` already exists in the audit log (M7). For assignment notifications, publish a domain event in the same transaction and deliver **after commit**, via the transactional outbox pattern (M9 explains it): an `outbox` row written with the change, a worker that sends and marks it sent. Spring Modulith's event publication registry implements exactly this; consider it.
- **SLA ageing:** a daily `NOTIFY_AGEING` job queries open exceptions past per-organization thresholds (`sla_hours` by exception type), and sends one digest per assignee, not one message per case.
- **Channels:** email first (SMTP via a provider), then Slack/Teams webhooks. A `NotificationChannel` interface; per-user preferences (`notification_preference`).
- **Idempotency:** a `notification_sent (key, sent_at)` table with a unique key such as `ASSIGNED:<exceptionId>:<version>` or `AGEING:<userId>:<date>`, so retries never double-send.

**Hard parts.**
- Never send from inside the business transaction: if the transaction rolls back, the email was still sent. That's the dual-write problem; the outbox solves it.
- Notification content must not leak financial details to channels that aren't access-controlled. Send a link, not the amounts.

**Interview angle.** "How do you send an email when a database change commits, without losing or duplicating it?" The transactional outbox: write the intent in the same transaction, deliver asynchronously with retries, deduplicate with an idempotency key.

---

### 3.10 A minimal web UI for exception investigation

**The business problem.** The API is complete, but finance users won't use `curl`. A small UI also makes the portfolio demo far more convincing.

**Design sketch.**
- **Scope:** login, exception list with filters, exception detail (both records side by side, notes, history), assign / resolve / reopen, run list with summary. Nothing else.
- **Stack choice** (ADR): a separate SPA (React + TypeScript + Vite) talking to the existing API, or server-rendered pages (Thymeleaf + htmx) inside the app. The SPA showcases API design and CORS/auth handling; htmx is far less code and keeps one deployable. For a backend-focused portfolio, **htmx is the pragmatic choice**; for a full-stack role, the SPA.
- **Auth:** with Keycloak (M10.1), use the authorization-code flow with PKCE for an SPA, or a session-backed login for server-rendered pages. Don't put tokens in `localStorage`.
- **Concurrency UX:** the API's `409 stale-version` needs a real UI answer: "this case was changed by Alice at 10:02, reload to see the changes". This is where M7's design pays off visibly.

**Hard parts.** Keeping it small. Every screen invites "just one more feature".

**Interview angle.** "How does your UI handle concurrent edits?" It sends the `ETag` back as `If-Match`, and on `409` shows who changed what and offers a reload, instead of retrying blindly.

---

### 3.11 Redis, only if a measured need appears

**The business problem.** Possibly none. docs/scalability.md decision D8 says: nothing needs caching at v1 scale. Report totals are precomputed at run completion, hot reads are indexed lookups, and coordination is done with PostgreSQL (`SKIP LOCKED`, advisory locks).

**When it would be justified** (docs/scalability.md §8 style: a measured signal, not a feeling):

| Signal | Possible Redis use | Alternative to try first |
|---|---|---|
| Rate limiting (M10.4) must be accurate across many API instances | Shared token buckets (Bucket4j has a Redis backend) | Rate limiting at the load balancer / API gateway |
| A repeated expensive read with a stable result shows up in the top slow queries | Cache with explicit TTL and invalidation on write | A materialized view or a precomputed summary table |
| Session storage for a server-rendered UI (§3.10) across instances | Spring Session with Redis | Spring Session JDBC in PostgreSQL |

**Rule.** Write the ADR with the measurement before adding it. Caching financial data adds an invalidation problem where stale data is a correctness bug, not just a UX issue.

**Interview angle.** "Why didn't you use Redis?" Because no measurement called for it: the database handles the load with headroom, report totals are precomputed, and adding a cache to financial data introduces a consistency risk. Then name the specific signal that would change the decision.

---

## 4. How to turn an item into a phase

When you pick an item up:

1. Write the `reconciliation-rules.md` change and the ADR. Get the rule right on paper with worked examples (tables like projectoverview.md §5).
2. Add sample CSVs for the new scenarios to `docs/samples/`.
3. Create `plans/phase-v2-<item>.plan` in the same 8-section format as M0–M11, with task IDs (`V2.1.1`, …) added to TASKS.md.
4. Extend, don't fork, the v1 invariants: every record in exactly one place per run, idempotent runs, one active case per discrepancy, audit in the same transaction. Add each new outcome to the M6.19 property test.
5. Update `docs/architecture.md`, `api-design.md` and the README roadmap when it ships.
